TITLE:: PltScatter
summary:: Scatter of xs against ys
categories:: Libraries>PlotLib
related:: Overviews/PlotLib, Classes/PltView, Classes/PltBifurcation, Classes/PltPhase

DESCRIPTION::
A great many small points in a box, with axes taken from the data. The base for
link::Classes/PltBifurcation:: and link::Classes/PltPhase::.

All the points go into one code::Pen.fill::: filling per point is far slower.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: xs
argument:: ys
Collections of the same length. Extra values in the longer one are ignored.
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: data_
Replace the data and refresh. The visible range is recomputed from it.

METHOD:: limits_
Fix the visible range instead of taking it from the data.
argument:: xLo
argument:: xHi
argument:: yLo
argument:: yHi

METHOD:: marker_
Draw a vertical line at this x value, for "the parameter is here". Refreshes, so it
can be dragged from a slider.

METHOD:: equalAspect
When true, both axes get the same units per pixel, by widening the tighter range
around its centre. Off by default: attractor figures are conventionally scaled to
fill the frame, and the tick labels make the scaling visible either way. Turn it on
when the true proportions of a shape matter.

METHOD:: dotColor
METHOD:: dotSize
METHOD:: alpha
Alpha below 1 is what makes density legible: where the orbit spends its time comes
out brighter.

METHOD:: markerColor
METHOD:: xs
METHOD:: ys

EXAMPLES::
code::
(
var xs = (0 .. 1200).collect { |i| sin(i * 0.031) };
var ys = (0 .. 1200).collect { |i| sin(i * 0.043 + 0.4) };
p = PltScatter(xs, ys, "Lissajous").dotSize_(2).alpha_(0.6).front;
)

p.limits_(-1, 1, -1, 1);
p.equalAspect_(true).refresh;
p.close;
::
